-- Q9 [Titration & Titration Curves · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Titration (GC) · general-chemistry · ans B] (g67)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Titration & Titration Curves',
    $qt67$A titration curve is recorded as a solution of unknown composition is titrated. The curve begins well up in the basic range, at a high \(\text{pH}\) far above 7, then falls only gradually through an early, nearly flat plateau as titrant is added and the dissolved species is steadily converted to its conjugate partner. After this buffer-like region the curve drops almost vertically through a narrow steep zone, and the equivalence point lands in the acidic range, below \(\text{pH}\) 7, because the species present at equivalence is a weak conjugate acid that donates protons to water. Which acid-base pairing produced this curve?$qt67$,
    $op67$[{"label":"A","text":"A strong acid titrated with a strong base"},{"label":"B","text":"A weak base titrated with a strong acid"},{"label":"C","text":"A weak acid titrated with a strong base"},{"label":"D","text":"A strong base titrated with a strong acid"}]$op67$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex67$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because every recorded feature matches a weak base being titrated with a strong acid. The solution starts in the basic range because the analyte in the flask is a weak base, giving an initial \(\text{pH}\) above 7. As strong acid is added, the weak base is progressively converted to its conjugate acid, and the coexistence of the weak base with its conjugate acid creates a buffer region that appears as the early, nearly flat plateau where \(\text{pH}\) changes only slowly. Past the steep region the equivalence point falls in the acidic range, below 7, because at equivalence essentially all of the weak base has been converted to its conjugate acid, and that conjugate acid undergoes the ionization \(\text{BH}^+ + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{B} + \text{H}_3\text{O}^+\), generating \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) and depressing the \(\text{pH}\) below 7. (Choice A) A strong acid titrated with a strong base would begin at a very low \(\text{pH}\) near 1, not in the basic range, would show no buffer plateau because neither species has a conjugate equilibrium, and its equivalence point would be neutral rather than acidic; this choice reverses the entire shape of the curve. (Choice C) A weak acid titrated with a strong base produces the mirror-image curve: it begins in the acidic range at low \(\text{pH}\), rises through a buffer plateau, and reaches an equivalence point in the basic range above 7 because the conjugate base hydrolyzes; the described curve runs in the opposite direction. (Choice D) A strong base titrated with a strong acid does begin in the basic range, which matches the start, but it lacks a buffer plateau and its equivalence point is neutral rather than acidic, so the basic starting point alone cannot account for the conjugate-acid equivalence in the acidic range. Skill 4$ex67$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc67$Titration (GC)$cc67$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
