-- Q10 [Titration & Titration Curves · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Titration (GC) · general-chemistry · ans B] (g68)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Titration & Titration Curves',
    $qt68$A titration curve is recorded for a solution of unknown composition. From the very start the \(\text{pH}\) changes only gradually; the curve is shallow throughout, and—unlike the other titration types—it shows no steep, near-vertical jump anywhere. Near the point where stoichiometrically equivalent amounts have reacted, the \(\text{pH}\) passes gently through a value close to neutral with only a slight, poorly defined inflection. Which acid-base combination produces a curve that lacks any sharp equivalence-point jump like this?$qt68$,
    $op68$[{"label":"A","text":"A strong acid titrated with a strong base"},{"label":"B","text":"A weak acid titrated with a weak base"},{"label":"C","text":"A weak acid titrated with a strong base"},{"label":"D","text":"A weak base titrated with a strong acid"}]$op68$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex68$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because only a weak-acid/weak-base titration lacks a sharp, near-vertical jump at the equivalence point. Both the analyte and the titrant are only partially ionized, so near the equivalence point neither a large excess of \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) nor of \(\text{OH}^-\) builds up; the \(\text{pH}\) changes only slightly and gradually, passing through a poorly defined inflection near neutral. This shallow shape, with no steep rise or fall, is exactly why weak/weak titrations are difficult to follow with an indicator. (Choice A) A strong acid titrated with a strong base gives the SHARPEST, tallest near-vertical jump of all, passing through \(\text{pH}\) 7 at equivalence — the opposite of the shallow, jumpless curve described. (Choice C) A weak acid titrated with a strong base does show a steep rise at equivalence (to a basic equivalence point well above 7) after its buffer region, so it has the sharp jump this curve lacks. (Choice D) A weak base titrated with a strong acid starts basic and drops through a steep jump to an acidic equivalence point, so it too has a sharp jump that the described curve does not. Skill 4$ex68$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc68$Titration (GC)$cc68$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a68A$expects the sharp strong/strong jump even for the shallow, jumpless curve described$a68A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a68C$weak acid + strong base does have a steep jump to a basic equivalence point$a68C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a68D$mirror case: weak base + strong acid has a steep jump to an acidic equivalence point$a68D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Titration & Titration Curves · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Titration (GC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g69)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Titration & Titration Curves',
    $qt69$A 0.10 M acetic acid solution (\(\text{p}K_a = 4.74\)) is titrated with 0.10 M NaOH, producing a curve that starts near \(\text{pH}\) 3, rises steeply at first, then enters a long region where the \(\text{pH}\) climbs only slowly and is flattest near \(\text{pH}\) 4.7 midway toward the equivalence point, after which it rises sharply through an equivalence point near \(\text{pH}\) 9 and then levels off again at high \(\text{pH}\). The titration possesses its greatest buffering capacity in the region where$qt69$,
    $op69$[{"label":"A","text":"the \\(\\text{pH}\\) rises most steeply through the equivalence point near \\(\\text{pH}\\) 9, because the largest change in \\(\\text{pH}\\) marks the point of greatest resistance to added base."},{"label":"B","text":"the very start of the titration before any base is added, because the solution there is essentially pure acetic acid and is therefore most able to neutralize added base."},{"label":"C","text":"the \\(\\text{pH}\\) is flattest near \\(\\text{pH}\\) 4.7 midway toward the equivalence point, because \\([\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}]\\) and \\([\\text{CH}_3\\text{COO}^-]\\) are comparable there so the \\(\\text{pH}\\) changes least per increment of base."},{"label":"D","text":"the curve levels off again at high \\(\\text{pH}\\) past the equivalence point, because a flat \\(\\text{pH}\\) there shows the solution is resisting further change from added base."}]$op69$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex69$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because buffering capacity is greatest where the titration curve is flattest before the equivalence point, and for a weak acid that flat plateau is centered on the half-equivalence point. Between the start and the equivalence point, added \(\text{OH}^-\) converts \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) into \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\), so the solution contains a mixture of the weak acid and its conjugate base, the defining condition of a buffer. By the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\frac{[\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-]}{[\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}]}\), the \(\text{pH}\) sits at \(\text{p}K_a = 4.74\) when the two species are present in equal amounts (the half-equivalence point), and near that point the log ratio changes only slightly per increment of base, so the \(\text{pH}\) barely moves. That minimal change in \(\text{pH}\) per added base is exactly what maximum buffering capacity means, which is why the described flat region near \(\text{pH}\) 4.7 is the answer. (Choice A) The steep rise through the equivalence point is the region of least buffering, not most: there the weak acid is nearly exhausted, so a small amount of added base produces a very large \(\text{pH}\) jump; this reverses the relationship between curve steepness and buffering capacity. (Choice B) At the very start the solution is almost pure acetic acid with very little conjugate base present, so the buffer ratio is far from 1 and the \(\text{pH}\) changes relatively rapidly with the first additions of base; a buffer requires comparable amounts of both members of the conjugate pair, which has not yet been established. (Choice D) The flat region past the equivalence point reflects excess strong base (\(\text{NaOH}\)) dominating the \(\text{pH}\), not a weak-acid/conjugate-base buffer; the curve is shallow there simply because \(\text{pH}\) is a logarithmic scale and added strong base changes \([\text{OH}^-]\) only modestly, so it does not represent buffering by the acetate system. Skill 4$ex69$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc69$Titration (GC)$cc69$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
