-- Q16 [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · easy · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g74)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch',
    $qt74$A formic-acid buffer is prepared containing 0.15 M formic acid (\(\text{HCOOH}\), \(\text{p}K_a = 3.75\)) and 0.15 M sodium formate (\(\text{HCOO}^-\)) in the same solution. What is the \(\text{pH}\) of this buffer?$qt74$,
    $op74$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 7.00\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 1.00\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 3.75\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 4.75\\)"}]$op74$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex74$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\!\left(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right)\), reduces to \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a\) whenever the conjugate base and weak acid are present at equal concentrations. Here \([\text{HCOO}^-] = [\text{HCOOH}] = 0.15\ \text{M}\), so the ratio \(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]} = \frac{0.15}{0.15} = 1\) and \(\log(1) = 0\). Therefore \(\text{pH} = 3.75 + \log(1) = 3.75 + 0 = 3.75\). Note that the buffer is acidic, not neutral, even though the two species are at equal concentration, because the \(\text{pH}\) is fixed by the acid's \(\text{p}K_a\). (Choice A) \(\text{pH} = 7.00\) assumes that equal concentrations of the acid and its conjugate base produce a neutral solution; in fact, equal concentrations make the log term zero so the \(\text{pH}\) equals the \(\text{p}K_a\) of the weak acid, which here is acidic at 3.75. (Choice B) \(\text{pH} = 1.00\) comes from taking the negative log of a concentration, e.g. \(-\log(0.15) \approx 0.8\) rounded toward 1, treating the buffer as if \([\text{H}^+]\) equaled a stated concentration rather than applying the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship. (Choice D) \(\text{pH} = 4.75\) is the result of adding 1 to the \(\text{p}K_a\) (\(3.75 + 1 = 4.75\)), an off-by-one error that would correspond to a tenfold excess of conjugate base over acid (\(\log 10 = 1\)) rather than the equal concentrations given. Skill 2$ex74$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc74$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc74$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
