-- Q17 [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · hard · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g75)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch',
    $qt75$A 1.00 L buffer initially holds 0.50 mol propionic acid (\(\text{HA}\), \(\text{p}K_a = 4.87\)) and 0.50 mol propionate (\([\text{A}^-]\)). A chemist adds 0.10 mol solid \(\text{NaOH}\), which reacts completely and converts that much \(\text{HA}\) to \(\text{A}^-\) (treat the volume change as negligible). What is the \(\text{pH}\) after the addition?$qt75$,
    $op75$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 4.87\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 4.69\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 5.65\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 5.05\\)"}]$op75$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex75$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the added strong base consumes \(\text{HA}\) and generates an equal amount of \(\text{A}^-\), shifting the conjugate ratio and raising the \(\text{pH}\) by \(\log(1.5)\). The strong base \(\text{NaOH}\) reacts essentially to completion: \(\text{HA} + \text{OH}^- \rightarrow \text{A}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O}\). It destroys 0.10 mol \(\text{HA}\) and creates 0.10 mol \(\text{A}^-\), so the new amounts are \(\text{HA} = 0.50 - 0.10 = 0.40\ \text{mol}\) and \([\text{A}^-] = 0.50 + 0.10 = 0.60\ \text{mol}\). Because both species share the same 1.00 L volume, the mole ratio equals the concentration ratio, and the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\!\left(\dfrac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right) = 4.87 + \log\!\left(\dfrac{0.60}{0.40}\right) = 4.87 + \log(1.5) = 4.87 + 0.18 = 5.05\). (Choice A) \(4.87\) is the value obtained by leaving the ratio at \(0.50/0.50 = 1\) so that \(\log(1) = 0\); this ignores that the added \(\text{OH}^-\) perturbs the buffer and changes the conjugate-base-to-acid ratio. (Choice B) \(4.69\) comes from inverting the ratio to \(\log(0.40/0.60) = -0.18\), placing the acid in the numerator and the base in the denominator; this reverses the direction of the shift, lowering rather than raising \(\text{pH}\) even though base was added. (Choice C) \(5.65\) results from using the 0.10 mol of added base as the new \(\text{HA}\) amount, giving \(\log(0.60/0.10) = \log(6) = 0.78\); this over-shifts the buffer by assuming nearly all of the acid was consumed instead of only 0.10 mol of the original 0.50 mol. Skill 2$ex75$,
    'hard', '5A', $cc75$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc75$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
