-- Q18 [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · hard · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans A] (g76)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch',
    $qt76$A 1.00 L buffer contains 0.40 mol \(\text{NH}_3\) and 0.40 mol \(\text{NH}_4^+\), with \(\text{p}K_a(\text{NH}_4^+) = 9.25\). After 0.10 mol \(\text{HCl}\) is added (no volume change), what is the \(\text{pH}\)?$qt76$,
    $op76$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 9.03\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 9.25\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 9.47\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.83\\)"}]$op76$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex76$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because the added strong acid is consumed by the weak base, shifting the buffer ratio toward the conjugate acid. The 0.10 mol \(\text{H}^+\) from \(\text{HCl}\) reacts essentially completely with \(\text{NH}_3\): \(\text{NH}_3 + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{NH}_4^+\). This consumes 0.10 mol \(\text{NH}_3\) and produces 0.10 mol \(\text{NH}_4^+\), giving new amounts \([\text{NH}_3] = 0.40 - 0.10 = 0.30\ \text{mol}\) and \([\text{NH}_4^+] = 0.40 + 0.10 = 0.50\ \text{mol}\). Applying Henderson-Hasselbalch with these new amounts (the 1.00 L volume cancels in the ratio): \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\!\left(\frac{[\text{NH}_3]}{[\text{NH}_4^+]}\right) = 9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{0.30}{0.50}\right) = 9.25 + \log(0.60) = 9.25 + (-0.22) = 9.03\). The \(\text{pH}\) drops slightly because adding acid increases the conjugate acid relative to the base. (Choice B) \(9.25\) is the result of ignoring the shift entirely and using the initial equal amounts, for which \(\log(0.40/0.40) = \log(1) = 0\); this skips the reaction of \(\text{H}^+\) with \(\text{NH}_3\). (Choice C) \(9.47\) comes from shifting the ratio in the wrong direction—treating the added acid as if it increased \(\text{NH}_3\) and decreased \(\text{NH}_4^+\), giving \(9.25 + \log(0.50/0.30) = 9.25 + 0.22\); adding acid cannot raise the \(\text{pH}\). (Choice D) \(8.83\) results from over-shifting the ratio, applying the \(0.10\ \text{mol}\) conversion twice (or doubling the log term to \(-0.44\)) so that the \(\text{pH}\) falls roughly twice as far as the correct single conversion warrants. Skill 2$ex76$,
    'hard', '5A', $cc76$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc76$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
