-- Q19 [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans B] (g77)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch',
    $qt77$A researcher must hold a reaction mixture at \(\text{pH} = 7.2\) and intends to prepare the buffer using roughly equal concentrations of a weak acid and its conjugate base. Four candidate weak acids are available, with \(\text{p}K_a\) values of \(3.1\) (acid W), \(4.8\) (acid X), \(7.2\) (acid Y), and \(9.3\) (acid Z). Which conjugate acid/base pair should be chosen to buffer most effectively at the target \(\text{pH}\)?$qt77$,
    $op77$[{"label":"A","text":"Acid W \\((\\text{p}K_a = 3.1)\\) with its conjugate base"},{"label":"B","text":"Acid Y \\((\\text{p}K_a = 7.2)\\) with its conjugate base"},{"label":"C","text":"Acid Z \\((\\text{p}K_a = 9.3)\\) with its conjugate base"},{"label":"D","text":"Acid X \\((\\text{p}K_a = 4.8)\\) with its conjugate base"}]$op77$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex77$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because a buffer made from roughly equal concentrations of weak acid and conjugate base buffers most effectively when its \(\text{p}K_a\) matches the target \(\text{pH}\). The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation is \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\left(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right)\). When \([\text{A}^-] \approx [\text{HA}]\), the ratio is about \(1\), so \(\log(1) = 0\) and \(\text{pH} \approx \text{p}K_a\). To hit \(\text{pH} = 7.2\) with the acid and base near a \(1{:}1\) ratio, the acid whose \(\text{p}K_a = 7.2\) (acid Y) is required, because that choice sets \(\text{pH} = 7.2 + \log(1) = 7.2\) exactly while staying in the high-capacity region where the buffer resists added acid or base most strongly. (Choice A) Acid W has \(\text{p}K_a = 3.1\); at a near \(1{:}1\) ratio it buffers near \(\text{pH} = 3.1\), and forcing it up to \(\text{pH} = 7.2\) would require \(\log\left(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right) = 4.1\), a ratio over \(10{,}000{:}1\) that lies far outside the effective buffering window and contradicts the stated near-equal concentrations. (Choice C) Acid Z has \(\text{p}K_a = 9.3\), which is 2.1 units above the target; a near \(1{:}1\) mixture buffers near \(\text{pH} = 9.3\), and reaching \(\text{pH} = 7.2\) would need \(\log\left(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right) = -2.1\), again far from the equal-concentration condition. (Choice D) Acid X has \(\text{p}K_a = 4.8\), the second-closest value, but \(4.8\) is still 2.4 units below \(7.2\); choosing it because it is "closer than the extremes" overlooks that only the acid whose \(\text{p}K_a\) equals the target gives \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a\) at a \(1{:}1\) ratio. Skill 4$ex77$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc77$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc77$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
