-- Q20 [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · easy · Skill 1 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g78)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch',
    $qt78$A buffer is prepared from equal concentrations of acetic acid \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) (\(K_a = 1.8 \times 10^{-5}\)) and its conjugate base acetate \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\). When a small amount of strong base \(\text{NaOH}\) is added, the \(\text{pH}\) rises only slightly. Which interaction keeps the \(\text{pH}\) nearly constant?$qt78$,
    $op78$[{"label":"A","text":"The added \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) is permanently destroyed by the buffer, so the system contains no hydroxide after mixing."},{"label":"B","text":"An acetate buffer contains no acidic species, so the added \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) has nothing to react with and the \\(\\text{pH}\\) is held by the salt alone."},{"label":"C","text":"The added \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) is consumed by the weak acid \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}\\), converting a strong base into the much weaker acetate \\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{COO}^-\\), so \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\) changes only slightly."},{"label":"D","text":"The water solvent neutralizes the added \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) on its own, so the dissolved acetic acid and acetate are not involved."}]$op78$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex78$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because a buffer resists \(\text{pH}\) change by having both members of a conjugate pair present, each able to consume an added strong species and convert it into a weak one. When \(\text{NaOH}\) is added, the hydroxide reacts with the weak acid: \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH} + \text{OH}^- \rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{COO}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O}\). The strong base \(\text{OH}^-\) is replaced by the weak conjugate base acetate \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\) (whose \(K_b = K_w/K_a = 1.0\times 10^{-14} / 1.8\times 10^{-5} \approx 5.6 \times 10^{-10}\), far weaker than \(\text{OH}^-\)). Because the \([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}]\) ratio shifts only modestly and \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\!\big([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}]\big)\) depends on the logarithm of that ratio, \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) and thus \(\text{pH}\) change only slightly. Were strong acid added instead, the conjugate base acetate would consume the \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) by the analogous reaction, giving the same buffering effect. (Choice A) Neutralization does not "destroy" \(\text{OH}^-\); the hydroxide is converted into \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and acetate through a real chemical reaction, and the system retains its conjugate pair in equilibrium rather than ending with no base. (Choice B) An acetate-containing buffer made from acetic acid does contain an acidic species, \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\); it is precisely that weak acid that reacts with the added \(\text{OH}^-\), so it is incorrect to claim nothing reacts. (Choice D) Pure water cannot neutralize an appreciable amount of added strong base while holding \(\text{pH}\) steady; without the weak acid \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) to consume the \(\text{OH}^-\), the \(\text{pH}\) would rise sharply, which is exactly why an un-buffered solution lacks this resistance. Skill 1$ex78$,
    'easy', '5A', $cc78$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc78$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
