-- Q21 [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · medium · Skill 4 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans D] (g79)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch',
    $qt79$A biochemist needs to prepare a buffer with a target \(\text{pH} = 5.04\) using a weak acid that has \(\text{p}K_a = 4.74\) and its conjugate base. Using the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation, what approximate ratio of conjugate base to weak acid, \([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}]\), must the biochemist establish in the solution?$qt79$,
    $op79$[{"label":"A","text":"\\([\\text{A}^-]/[\\text{HA}] \\approx 10\\) (about \\(10:1\\))"},{"label":"B","text":"\\([\\text{A}^-]/[\\text{HA}] \\approx 1.0\\) (about \\(1:1\\))"},{"label":"C","text":"\\([\\text{A}^-]/[\\text{HA}] \\approx 0.5\\) (about \\(1:2\\))"},{"label":"D","text":"\\([\\text{A}^-]/[\\text{HA}] \\approx 2.0\\) (about \\(2:1\\))"}]$op79$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex79$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The Henderson–Hasselbalch equation states that \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\!\left(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right)\). Rearranging to solve for the ratio gives \(\log\!\left(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\right) = \text{pH} - \text{p}K_a\), so \(\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]} = 10^{(\text{pH} - \text{p}K_a)}\). The answer to this question is D because \(\text{pH} - \text{p}K_a = 5.04 - 4.74 = 0.30\), and \(10^{0.30} \approx 2.0\); the conjugate base must be present at roughly twice the concentration of the weak acid (about \(2:1\)) to push the pH 0.30 unit above the \(\text{p}K_a\). (Choice A) A ratio of \(10\) corresponds to \(10^{1}\), which would require the pH to lie a full unit (1.00) above the \(\text{p}K_a\), not the 0.30 unit that the actual target pH demands. (Choice B) A \(1:1\) ratio gives \(\log(1) = 0\), so the pH would equal the \(\text{p}K_a\) of 4.74; this choice ignores the required \(+0.30\) offset between the target pH and the \(\text{p}K_a\). (Choice C) A ratio of \(0.5\) corresponds to \(10^{-0.30}\), which would arise from computing \(\text{p}K_a - \text{pH}\) instead of \(\text{pH} - \text{p}K_a\), inverting the ratio and yielding a pH below the \(\text{p}K_a\) rather than above it. Skill 4$ex79$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc79$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc79$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
