-- Q22 [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · medium · Skill 2 · 5A Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC) · general-chemistry · ans C] (g80)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Acids & Bases', 'Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch',
    $qt80$Two acetate buffers are prepared so that each is exactly equimolar in \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) and \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\) (a \(1:1\) conjugate ratio), and both therefore start at the same \(\text{pH}\) equal to the \(\text{p}K_a\) of \(4.74\). Buffer X is \(0.50\,\text{M}\) in each member of the pair, while Buffer Y is \(0.050\,\text{M}\) in each. The same small amount of strong acid (well below the moles of conjugate base in either solution) is added to each buffer. Which buffer undergoes the smaller change in \(\text{pH}\), and why?$qt80$,
    $op80$[{"label":"A","text":"Neither; both change by the same amount, because they share the same \\(\\text{p}K_a\\), the same \\(1:1\\) ratio, and therefore the same starting \\(\\text{pH}\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"Buffer Y, because its lower concentration of the conjugate pair makes the solution more reactive and therefore more sensitive in neutralizing the added acid."},{"label":"C","text":"Buffer X, because its higher total concentration of the conjugate pair gives it a larger reserve to neutralize the added acid before the conjugate ratio shifts appreciably."},{"label":"D","text":"Buffer X, but only if its \\(\\text{p}K_a\\) is higher than that of Buffer Y, since a larger \\(\\text{p}K_a\\) is what sets the greater resistance to the added acid."}]$op80$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex80$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because buffer capacity—the amount of added acid or base a buffer can absorb before its \(\text{pH}\) shifts substantially—increases with the total concentration of the conjugate acid-base pair. Both buffers begin at the same \(\text{pH}\) of \(4.74\) because the Henderson-Hasselbalch relation \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}])\) depends only on the \(\text{p}K_a\) and the ratio, both of which are identical here. When the same small number of moles of strong acid is added, it converts the same number of moles of \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\) to \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) in each beaker. In Buffer X, that fixed change is a much smaller fraction of the large \(0.50\,\text{M}\) reserves, so the \([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}]\) ratio barely moves and the \(\text{pH}\) changes very little. In Buffer Y, the identical change is a much larger fraction of the small \(0.050\,\text{M}\) amounts, so the ratio—and hence the \(\text{pH}\)—shifts more. The concentrated Buffer X therefore has the greater buffer capacity and undergoes the smaller \(\text{pH}\) change. (Choice A) is wrong because it confuses the factors that set the starting \(\text{pH}\) (the \(\text{p}K_a\) and the conjugate ratio) with the factor that sets buffer capacity (the absolute concentrations); two buffers can share an identical \(\text{pH}\) yet have very different capacities. (Choice B) is wrong because it reverses the actual relationship: a more dilute buffer holds fewer moles of conjugate base to consume the incoming \(\text{H}^+\), so it is overwhelmed sooner and its \(\text{pH}\) shifts more, not less; "more reactive" is not a meaningful basis for capacity. (Choice D) is wrong because the \(\text{p}K_a\) values are stated to be identical—both are acetate buffers—so \(\text{p}K_a\) cannot be the discriminating factor; capacity here is governed by concentration alone, not by any difference in \(\text{p}K_a\). Skill 2$ex80$,
    'medium', '5A', $cc80$Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)$cc80$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
